The outer layer is rotated first, then the next inner layer, and so on. For each position on a layer, four values are moved at once: top to right, right to bottom, bottom to left, and left to top.

The empty matrix and a one-cell matrix need no work, so the loop naturally handles them. This also changes the given matrix instead of creating another one.

```java
class Solution {
    public void rotateMatrix(int[][] matrix) {
        int n = matrix.length;

        for (int layer = 0; layer < n / 2; layer++) {
            int first = layer;
            int last = n - 1 - layer;

            for (int offset = 0; offset < last - first; offset++) {
                int top = matrix[first][first + offset];

                matrix[first][first + offset] = matrix[last - offset][first];
                matrix[last - offset][first] = matrix[last][last - offset];
                matrix[last][last - offset] = matrix[first + offset][last];
                matrix[first + offset][last] = top;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because every cell is moved once.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The helper handles one square ring at a time. It rotates the four corners of each position, then calls itself for the smaller ring inside. When the starting index reaches the middle, all rings are finished.

A dry run on `[ [1, 2], [3, 4] ]` swaps the four values once and gives `[ [3, 1], [4, 2] ]`. The input array is modified directly.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public void RotateMatrix(int[][] matrix) {
        RotateLayer(matrix, 0, matrix.Length);
    }

    private void RotateLayer(int[][] matrix, int start, int size) {
        if (size <= 1) {
            return;
        }

        int end = start + size - 1;

        for (int offset = 0; offset < size - 1; offset++) {
            int top = matrix[start][start + offset];

            matrix[start][start + offset] = matrix[end - offset][start];
            matrix[end - offset][start] = matrix[end][end - offset];
            matrix[end][end - offset] = matrix[start + offset][end];
            matrix[start + offset][end] = top;
        }

        RotateLayer(matrix, start + 1, size - 2);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursive calls.

This uses the same four-position idea as rotating a square ring. The top-left value is saved, and the other three positions fill the empty spot one by one.

The method works in place, so no second matrix is needed. A one by one matrix and an empty matrix do not enter the swap loops.

