A short in-place version works well here. First transpose the matrix, then reverse every row. After those two steps, each value moves to its clockwise position.

For example, `[1, 2, 3]` becomes the first column after transposing, and reversing the rows puts it in the correct first row. The input matrix is changed directly.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void rotateMatrix(vector<vector<int>>& matrix) {
        int n = matrix.size();

        for (int row = 0; row < n; ++row) {
            for (int col = row + 1; col < n; ++col) {
                swap(matrix[row][col], matrix[col][row]);
            }
        }

        for (int row = 0; row < n; ++row) {
            reverse(matrix[row].begin(), matrix[row].end());
        }
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the input matrix.

